# dao.stream.serve — review of Fable's P2P-invariant revision (GLM-5.3, round 1)

Verdict up front: **the dissolution is sound — relay and rendezvous really are derivable as ordinary peers serving ordinary streams, and "nothing privileged remains" is true at the protocol level. The mechanism has two real defects (one decisive: the punch socket rule) and one privacy regression.** Of the revised positions: I AGREE on Q2 and Q3 (new evidence, not convergence), AGREE on Q4 with one addition; the earlier consensus should not stand for Q2/Q3 as written. Q1, Q5, Q6, Q7 are genuinely unaffected. I verified every section-5 citation, including `rpc.cljc` `server-state`/`serve-once!` (`src/cljc/dao/stream/rpc.cljc:100,106` — per-session roles, no change needed, as Fable says), `dao.stream.discovery.md:119-125`, and `dao.jing.dht.md:163-167`.

## 1. The mechanism (section 3)

**Sound and derivable.** I traced every element to the contract: a writer-only served stream (surface restriction, findings.md:91-99), a reader-only single-writer served stream, appends through ordinary sessions (concurrent writers linearize by the source's sequence, findings.md:278), reads cursor-keyed and idempotent so UDP loss/reorder at the meeting layer is absorbed by the existing design (findings.md:109-117) — Fable's claim that an evicting inbox yields inner `gap`s that the inner retry absorbs checks out, because inner requests/responses are re-derivable by idempotence. The revised frame set (open/opened/disclaim/close/request/response/ping/pong) hides no node kind, and "every peer runs both steps" plus the owner's rulings (conventions are not privileges; dialer/acceptor is an establishment fact) close the invariant. Criterion 5's inversion (a dial-only peer serving through an inbox on a dial-only third peer, whose table holds "two ring buffers and no other state about the session") is correct. The double framing is stated honestly. **"Nothing privileged remains" is true as claimed** — the residual facts (M must be reachable; M chose the convention) are, per the owner's ruling (1), not privileges.

**Defect 1 — decisive: the punch must come from the same socket that produced the published reflexive address.** Revised §7.4 says each party "`attach!`es a UDP channel" to the other's reflexive address and pings. But `attach!` binds a **fresh local socket** (findings.md:192), and a NAT mapping exists per internal endpoint — a new local port means a new external mapping under **every** NAT type, not just symmetric ones. So the address M published (the mapping of the socket that talked to M) is stale on arrival; the ping that reaches the peer comes from a mapping it was never told, and punching as drafted succeeds only where the *receiving* side's filtering is permissive (full-cone). Classic STUN/ICE works for all cone NATs precisely because checks are sent from the same socket whose mapping was observed. Two required edits: (a) §7.4 — punch from the channel used to reach M, i.e. through the **same socket** whose mapping `:meet/seen` reported; (b) §6.3 — the udp seam must allow sending to an explicit destination on an existing socket, the direct channel being the new attachment that socket acquires when the peer's ping arrives. And criterion 6's NAT simulator must be **restricted-cone**; a full-cone simulator would pass a degraded implementation.

**Defect 2 — the reflexive correlation is glossed.** §7.3 says `:meet/seen` is "from `:udp/attachment` on the request," but the attachment identity lives on the **channel's traffic medium**, and the appended request value carries no address; the serve layer "interprets operations but never values" (findings.md:273), so nothing stamps it automatically. The meeting interpreter must correlate the appended request with the channel event **by session identity** (both media carry it; sessions are `[opener-peer-id n]`). One sentence fixes it (wording in §4 below).

**Regression Fable's table omits — privacy.** The original `:serve/introduce` was addressed to the two peers (findings.md:232); the revision publishes every peer's reflexive address (`:meet/seen`) as facts on a shared, publicly readable announcement stream — a home IP:port exposure to every reader of M's stream. Minimum fix: state it in the honest-costs line; better fix at zero mechanism cost: M delivers the other party's reflexive via each peer's own inbox (`:meet/caller <id> :meet/reflexive {…}` appended to the called peer's `in`), so the shared stream carries only peer ids.

**Two smaller requirements.** (i) `:meet/here` is an append — non-idempotent; a lost response (`:serve/append-unknown`, findings.md:115) followed by a re-request on a fresh session can double the inbox. The convention must state the re-read discipline: on unknown, re-read announcements from a cursor minted **before** the append (the contract's mint-before-invoke rule, `dao.stream.md:496-499`) before re-appending. (ii) The announcement stream's retention must exceed intended reader lag (the same sizing relation as `dao.lease.md:259`), or a lagging peer gaps over its own inbox fact and reachability discovery silently degrades.

## 2. Q2, Q3, Q4

**Q2 — AGREE with the revision; the consensus position should not stand.** This is an evidence-mover, not a concession. My deferral rested on "network chatter is a policy knob": in the original design a dialed channel **pushes** inbound frames into a local medium, so a parked reader polls host-locally and only its own demands cost wire. The revision removes that for NAT'd peers: a peer reachable through M has no pushed channel — its inbound path is a proxy over a serve session, so **reachability itself is a standing poll of the inbox proxy**, and inbound-connection latency is bounded by poll cadence. Held reads are the only mechanism in the protocol that lets a server answer later without a new request, turning per-retry traffic into per-budget traffic and restoring push-like delivery over a poll protocol. Two precisions: hold state is per-session and dies with the session (the proxy's demand machinery re-issues — state this, per findings.md:131's session scoping); and the timing should be definite, matching Fable's own second horn — held reads land **with the meeting-convention milestone** (criteria 5-6), not necessarily with serve-core loopback/direct (criteria 1-4, 7, 9 need none of it).

**Q3 — AGREE with the revision.** "Composition-assigned" (my phrase in the mob, reading findings.md:284) presupposed an assigner; with no relay there is none, and each peer's own composition assigning its id *is* self-minting — the revision disambiguates correctly rather than overturning. Self-minted 128-bit-random minimum makes collisions negligible; hash-of-public-key as the intended format aligns with `dao.stream.discovery.md:70-80` mechanism 1 and with the mob's own r2/r3 trajectory (verification is the authenticated channel's registration handshake, additive). Duplicate `:meet/here` as an ordinary fact handled by M's convention matches the mob's duplicate-registration rule, relocated to where an authority no longer exists. One caveat that must ride along: until the additive verification lands, a self-minted id is still an **unauthenticated name** (`dao.stream.md:1087-1092`) — the intended format must not be read as delivering authentication, and the honest-costs line should say so.

**Q4 — AGREE; the consensus substance survives restated.** No relay to admit, but the consensus's content maps exactly: admission is M's interpreter rule over its own request medium (the contract gates nothing, `dao.stream.md:285-292`), caps and lifecycle become "M bounds the number and retention of inboxes it serves," postage remains the intended answer for a public meeting stream (`dao.stream.discovery.md:158-162`, design unspecified at `:231-233`). Add the announcement-retention sizing sentence (item 1(ii) above) to the same policy family.

## 3. Q1, Q5, Q6, Q7

**Q1 — unaffected, confirmed.** Append semantics, the event-medium outcome, and the UCF `:put` amendment are untouched; a source outcome displaced through a relayed channel is still "the observed source outcome," just observable later.

**Q5 — unaffected, confirmed — and reinforced.** The proxy's cached-read semantics are identical, and the revision multiplies the case the amended definitions were written for: an inbox-channel reader is a proxy whose channel legs are themselves proxies, i.e. deferred observation all the way down. The consensus amendment text (orchestrator file, lines 24-36) needs no change.

**Q6 — unaffected, with one note.** Exporter and resumer are per-migration roles, covered by the owner's convention clause. The note: the UCF migration acceptance's "adequate lifetime" now spans **two** reclamation layers — the exporter's served entry (lease-governed) *and* M's idle-inbox policy; a resumer can lose reachability because M reclaimed, not because a lease lapsed, and it surfaces as a failed candidate / `:serve/disclaim`, not a lease fact. One sentence in §9 or criterion 8, so the acceptance test fixes M's policy for the duration.

**Q7 — unaffected, strengthened as Fable says.** The retitle of §5, deletion of the relay/rendezvous sections, and re-describing the copy composition as a broadcast convention are all in the consensus's direction.

## 4. Edits to sections 5 and 6 (exact wording)

1. **§7.4, replace the first sentence:** "After a call is announced, each party punches **through the same UDP channel it used to reach M** — the socket whose mapping `:meet/seen` reported — sending `:serve/ping` to the other's reflexive address on the schedule its driver's `now` allows. The udp channel seam therefore permits sending to an explicit destination on an existing socket, and the direct channel is the attachment that socket acquires when the other's ping arrives. **A fresh `attach!` mints a fresh socket and therefore a fresh NAT mapping under every NAT type; punching from a new socket succeeds only against permissive (full-cone) filtering and is not this design's punch.** No pong within the punch budget means the next candidate…" — and in §13 criterion 6, after "NAT simulator," insert "(**restricted-cone**, so the same-socket rule is load-bearing)".
2. **§7.3, add after the `:meet/seen` fact:** "The reflexive address of a request is the channel attachment identity its session arrived on; the meeting interpreter correlates the appended request with that attachment by session identity, which the channel's medium and the request both carry, and publishes the pair. A requester whose `:meet/here` ends `append-unknown` re-reads the announcement stream from a cursor minted before the append before re-appending, so an inbox is not doubled. The announcement stream's retention exceeds the lag of the readers M intends to serve."
3. **§7.3 honest costs, add:** "A relayed value is framed twice, a reachable peer holds one demand open against its inbox, and `:meet/seen` facts on a shared announcement stream expose reflexive addresses to every reader — a convention may instead deliver the caller's reflexive through the called peer's own inbox — and until channel authentication exists, a peer id is an unauthenticated name (`dao.stream.md`, *Recorded, not proposed*)."
4. **§7.1, third candidate kind, one-word consistency fix** (the inventory row says punching is "a resolution rule… not a candidate kind"): call it "a candidate whose resolution is the meeting procedure of §7.3," so the candidate list and the inventory agree.
5. **Section 5 table, add two rows:** (high) original design §7.4 `:serve/introduce` was addressed to the two peers (findings.md:232) while the revision publishes `:meet/seen` to all readers — the privacy regression above; (medium) §7.4 punch socket — the same-socket rule, without which criterion 6 is satisfiable by a degraded implementation.

I hold these on evidence, not to converge: the mechanism is the right shape, the invariant reading is faithful to the owner's rulings, and the defects above are fixable by the wording given without reopening any settled question.

CONSENSUS (my positions this round): mechanism sound with the five edits; Q2 revised-include (meeting-convention milestone), Q3 self-minted, Q4 restated — consensus Q2/Q3 superseded, Q4 substance survives; Q1/Q5/Q6/Q7 unaffected.
